\startsection[title={Algorithms for scRNA-seq Analysis}]

\startsubsection[title={BART-Seq}]
My work focused on reusable open source code in the form of a Python package and an accompanying \n{Snakemake} pipeline \cite[koster2012].
\stopsubsection

\stopsection

% end of section – sec:workflow

Apart from implementing and extending algorithms for analysis and visualization, I especially focused on improving the interoperability between such steps.
Before addressing my work in this field, I would like to outline the relevant changes in the process of scRNA-Seq analysis in the following sections.

% section – sec:org

\TODO[too close to sec:org’s intro?]
Starting in 2015, I identified a high amount of tasks being repeatedly rebuilt by scientists.
I noticed road blocks that were re-discovered and re-solved by my peers.
I consequently focused my work on improving interoperability and modularity of existing tools, pioneering technologies and software development processes in a research environment, and developed some of the missing building blocks for the resulting toolkit.

To demonstrate the need for rigorous software solutions like mine, I also analyzed sizes and sequencing depth of 455 publicly available datasets and the impact of growing data sizes (see \infig[fig:dataset-sizes]) on the field \cite[angerer2017].
The proposed ramifications of these changes are gradually becoming reality,
such as the use of transfer learning to infer labels for new data from multimodal data in cell atlases \cite[abdelaal2019].

